This is a “Compelling Story” speech based on your personal experience. It
allows you to exercise your storytelling skills. Ch. 12 and Ch. 14 are
foundational for this assignment.

1. Selecting a topic. Think of a personal experience that was significant in
   your life. It may have been frightening, inspiring, embarrassing, funny,
   adventuresome, etc. Select something that will be meaningful and appropriate
   to share with the audience of your peers.
   - This does not require any outside research or reading, but DO think
     through your story and know how you will present it.

2. Defining purpose and thesis. Define your general and specific purposes and
   form a thesis. Include them in your outline.
   - Write these in full sentences.

3. Key-Word Outline. Use a key-word outline to organize your thoughts.
   Following the textbook format, upload it to the Dropbox on eClass before
   your speech.
   - Follow the textbook guidelines for its format.

4. Speech Organization. Use the speech organizing guidelines you learned so
   far:
   - Introduction & Conclusion. Write a creative introduction (the “hook,”
     thesis, and preview) and bring the narrative to an end with a strong
     conclusion tied to the introduction. You must apply a principle, lesson,
     or moral to your story in conclusion.
   - Body. Write a logical body (narrow down – stay on target, from the problem
     to solution/culmination; no more than 3-4 main points), and choose an
     appropriate organizational pattern.
   - Use effective transitions between parts, simple sentences, and appropriate
     redundancy.
   - Don’t exceed your time limit (3-4 mins).

5. Language. Use sensory descriptions, vivid details, and strong verbs.
   Describe the twists and turns that led to the situation and share your
   takeaways. Remember that conflict, characterization, and descriptive detail
   make for high-impact narratives. Ensure there are smooth transitions between
   speech parts and main points.

6. After preparing your speech, write out a transcript of your introduction and
   conclusion. Write a prompt for the AI to give you feedback about the
   effectiveness of your intro and conclusion. Adjust your speech as necessary.
   - Add the prompt(s) you used to the outline.

7. Delivery. It’s an extemporaneous presentation – no reading or memorization.
   Aim for strong eye contact (scanning the room while speaking) and avoid
   distracting mannerisms (fidgeting, filler words "um," "like,” etc., overuse
   of gestures, repeating phrases, etc.).

8. Dress: business casual (no shorts, T-shirts, sportswear, tight closing,
   jewelry, hats, etc.).

9. Speech Notes. No Digital Devices. Use 4x6 index cards or a half sheet of
   paper. Use for key points to trigger your memory; don’t read from them.

